[Music]  I think through a lot of different  factors I came together for stage 19 to  to really take place the start of the  Giro over in Jerusalem I had crashed in  the the Recon of the time trial and  before the race even begun some crashes  you get up from and brush it off and  you're fine but this one I wasn't right  I wasn't right and I felt for at least  the first 10 days of the race I just  wasn't myself I felt out of balance out  of synch and I I was hemorrhaging time I  was I was losing time at every  opportunity pretty much lets across we  knew that copy crashes Chris Froome is  down Chris room has had a torrid opening  to this talk I found myself coming into  stage 19 over three minutes down and I  knew it would take something absolutely  crazy to turn the race upside down and  it would be a kind of move of almost a  suicide move would be all or nothing  that idea of going solo ATK's out was a  decision I made in the morning before  the race I sent a message to my coach in  Karason and just said to him it's a bit  of a bit of a crazy move but what do you  think if I went ATK's out there's only  three climbs two descents it's going to  be a Time Trial and behind that they  could have to sort out a really good  chase if if they want to catch me I mean  and he replied and his reply was a  little bit along the lines of he didn't  say no but he said listen it would take  a lot of work to do something like that  I mean it was it was going to be a one  of the biggest days on the bike in my  life in terms of energy expenditure so  how do I feel for a stage like that I  had to think about how much fuelling I  need to get in before the race and then  again once I got into the race  how often do I feel and  on what and basically going at a time  trial as hard as I could certainly that  word out it's something that could go  completely wrong and you know what if it  doesn't work out then you lose  everything  but on the other side I was also  thinking about it as well I'm feeling  good I'm feeling better in myself now  I'm gonna get out there and go into time  trial mode as things developed out on  the road Simon Yates had started  dropping easy right near the back yeah  extra cozy that's not good that's not  good we hit the foot of the the finestra  and the team I asked the guys to ramp it  up through all the hair pins earlier on  and make the race harder and harder so  for the guys further back there would  have been quite tough already they're  racing we start they're not just riding  tempo they're actually they're attacking  they are bracing this climb at the top  on the hardest section now Yates was  dropped through Milan had started losing  teammates already  and I think only at that point that I  did I really believe okay I'm gonna do  this now this is the moment it's now or  never  through now he goes on the offensive he  goes on the attack over the top of this  final climate  it's just an amazing feeling to see that  do my line and the other TC guys they  want on their that that was that was  where the the time trial started four  times a winner of the Tour de France  winner of the world took a team every  year to do a d'Italia victory once I  accelerated and I saw no one come with  me I just stuff it I'm gonna go for it  now all or nothing and get stuck into  this and even on this descent he's  taking it  I wouldn't say I was taking risks on the  descents but I pushed on and went as  fast as I could  it was just an amazing feeling to get to  the bottom of the descent into the next  Valley and just hear that the gap it  opened up another 30 40 seconds just in  which is almost for free like this is  not really much pedaling to do on its  end it's more about the technique in the  lines you take as the race went on and  that that gap went up from one minute in  one a half to two and a half three and  it was just like wow I'm now in in in  reach of pink jersey and the overall  lead of the race Chris Froome is now the  virtual leader the valley of Rosa of  this jewett Italian what a stage it has  been being out in front has changed he  gave me even more I guess to to fight  for there is the boat Amalia motor on  the road  at the start of today what  today was about fuelling and today was  about making sure that you can fuel a  ride like this all the way through to  the end another is fundamental and so  all the members are staff every single  one of them myself included they now  decide their own and put in place a  fuelling plan for him to make sure that  he would absolutely not miss a beat  because that that's basically the game  changer it's certainly not easy to take  on that amount of carbohydrates every  opportunity I had I'll be putting  something else in my mouth and I think  the beta fuel played a huge part in that  obviously it's it's just a drink it's in  a bottle but you get 80 grams of carbs  from one bottle and I think I had two  beautiful drinks are on the road and I  literally went into the stage so full  already I think I was just just bursting  I felt as if I I literally cannot eat  any more right now but thankfully that's  the way I went into the race because  certainly when when I crossed that  finish line at the end I was completely  empty I had nothing left so I'm pretty  glad that I did fill up in that way  Chris Froome is gonna ride into Rome in  the pictures of the dirt this picture of  me is completely unique I've never  attacked it to gaze out and I mean it's  just something that you don't really see  very often anymore in professional  cycling it's just been a couple decades  since we since we saw someone win a  Grand Tour in that kind of fashion this  is the moment that we've been waiting  for Chris Froome is the winner of the  hundred and first edition of the Giudice  [Applause]  [Music]

